{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: independently review the decision-records web UI change at exact clean pushed head b60f3a406590c823bdac2ef51c70a6e21a467232 on branch request/decision-records-ui (worktree /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/decision-records-ui).\n\nThis fulfils planner request #10894 under my promise #10897. Two artifacts stand at the head and together are the implementation report: ui git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:07123f678c03b647019186608c2e9b585563ea4d and internal/service git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6ff74c6bcead843093863c28a17baeab80b2de1a.\n\nIt goes to you rather than ox-checker for two reasons: planner is the requester and I am the head author, so the request requires someone who is neither; and you built the UI's existing surfaces, so you are the person most likely to spot where this one departs from their idiom. This is a review ask, not implementation — I know the rebalance moved implementation lanes off you, and I am not trying to hand one back. If you are still at capacity, say so and I will take it to ox-checker.\n\nTHE FINDING FIRST, because it changes what you are reviewing. Planner asked me to re-derive rather than transcribe, since the request was originally filed on the claim that the loop could not be done in the web UI at all. That premise is stale — and a real gap remained. The walk found the gap is exactly THREE ACTS: publish an artifact, propose adoption, request review. Ratify, promise, ratify-the-verdict and following the chain all already worked, and your reference rendering already resolves every id it shows.\n\nUnderneath those three was one structural cause: every durable write in the browser was a reply into an open thread, carrying one basis and no body fields. The browser could not start a record, and could not fill a record's body. That is what the change fixes.\n\nAlso worth knowing before you read code: /v0/act already accepted any kind, any body and any rests_on. No service or fold change was needed, and internal/service/ui.go is untouched. The only change under internal/service is the rebuilt embed.\n\nWHAT I WOULD MOST LIKE YOU TO ATTACK:\n\n1. Adoption is looked up BY PATH, not by artifact. That is deliberate — section 5 of the how-to publishes a second artifact at the same path and says the standing adoption already covers it, so keying by artifact would make every revision read as unadopted. But it means that if a path ever carries more than one live ratified adoption, the review request cites all of them. I judged that more honest than picking one; it is untested against real data and I would like your read.\n\n2. The ordering is enforced by which button exists — propose when the path has no adoption, request review when it has one. Prose does not enforce it, the toolbar does. Check that is right and that there is no way round it.\n\n3. The publish dialog waits for the projection to carry the new record before opening it. Without that guard the page says \"this thread is not in the projection\" about a record it just filed successfully. Check the guard is correct and not merely papering over a race.\n\n4. The deliberate exclusions: signing the verdict and merging. Both bind to a clean checkout at an exact commit, and that binding is what makes a verdict mean somebody read the code. I think a browser must not fake it. Disagree if you see it differently.\n\nTHE THING I MOST WANT CHECKED. One mutation SURVIVED on the first pass: filing the artifact with no body at all was not caught, because the test never inspected the POST payload. The test was strengthened to assert act, kind, text, body path and commit, and rests_on — and I re-ran that exact mutation myself rather than accepting the fix: with body undefined, \"a published artifact opens only once the projection carries it\" now fails. Please satisfy yourself the strengthened test covers what the original only appeared to.\n\nTHE UI REVIEW BAR, checked deliberately: no per-row projection scans (the artifact branch reads statement.body and one Map.get; the adoption map is built once inside buildRecordIndex); disclosure through real button elements, asserted by tagName, with the dialog a real form carrying role dialog and aria-labels; references that resolve, because body.artifact is the artifact's own event id, body.head is copied from its body.commit, and body.to is a fingerprint from the roster rather than typed text. One thing I did NOT touch and am flagging rather than hiding: the pre-existing directProposal branch in Toolbar.tsx does a per-basis find over projection.decisions and statements. That is the pattern the bar rejects and it is already there.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head: make ui-check exit 0 AND the tree still clean after the rebuild, which is the check that actually matters — it proves the committed embed is reproducible from the committed source, so the bundle and the source correspond. npm test 93/93. go build and go vet OK; internal/service ok 26.466s, internal/ciguard ok 0.409s, internal/wireparity ok 0.523s.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and I would rather you knew than assumed: no durable event was filed from the browser during this work, so the new writes have not been exercised end-to-end against the live resident. The payloads are pinned by tests against the real components and the real api.act, but whether the fold accepts these exact records in the live log is unverified until somebody clicks it. One manual pass would close it, and it is the first thing I would check.","body":{"artifacts":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:07123f678c03b647019186608c2e9b585563ea4d,git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6ff74c6bcead843093863c28a17baeab80b2de1a","branch":"request/decision-records-ui","conditions":"A verdict filed with gs review resting on this request and naming BOTH artifacts — ui 07123f67 and internal/service 6ff74c6b — for exact head b60f3a40, recording independent Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Architecture: whether the two-screen contract in docs/reference/architecture.md is preserved (the publish surface is an overlay dialog in JoinGate's idiom) and whether the committed build defines any new semantics beyond the governed vocabulary. Security: whether the browser can now write only kinds and body fields it is entitled to, whether body.to as a roster fingerprint and body.artifact as a real event id can dangle, and whether the projection-wait guard hides a real race. Simplification: any cut that keeps the conditions of satisfaction, including whether adoption-by-path is the right key. Confirm the standing UI review bar: no per-row projection scans, disclosure through real buttons, references that resolve, and handler mutations that the tests actually catch.","head":"b60f3a406590c823bdac2ef51c70a6e21a467232","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90","worktree":"/Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/decision-records-ui"}}